The deployment's own instruments, read live tonight:
  boot CPU profile   'busy 14078ms idle 45962ms - top: fsync 78% 10979ms'
  JUDGE FUNNEL 01:45 'LOOP 119 stalls over 100 ms (worst 2526 ms, 46161 ms blocked)'
                     'DB tx p50 320 / p95 10892 / max 20346 ms (statements/tx 4, bytes/tx 0 KB)'
                     'pool checkout wait max 213 ms (failures 0)'
                     'journal commit p50 547 / p95 40850 / max 48629 ms'
                     'decision compute max 30914 ms' · 'top refusals ... PROPOSAL_EXPIRED_10S 2'

Four statements carrying ZERO kilobytes took twenty seconds while the pool handed out a connection in
one millisecond. The database was not slow — the process could not come back to it, because it was
inside fsync, writing every book update, trade and event synchronously as it arrived (240,017 book
updates in eighteen minutes).

judge/judge.js gives a hypothesis lock proposalExpiryMs (10 s) to become durable, and expires the
proposal past the same 10 s. A 40-second journal commit therefore makes an entry impossible however
good the setup is. That is a far better explanation of a zero-bite record than any gate.

Ticket B built and tested the write-behind buffer for exactly this and left it opt-in behind
SERPENT_TAPE_WRITE_BEHIND. Nothing ever turned it on. The paper profile's tape row now carries
writeBehind: true and profileEnvironment derives the flag from that row (absent means off, never
implied). A flush error never reaches the bite lane; tape/run.js drains it on a clean stop.

The 10-second proposal law is NOT relaxed — the fix is the disk, not the deadline. No policy byte
changed; the fee080 account binding is untouched.

Tests: publish-fix-97-io-lane-on 3/3 (FIX97-1 fails on the previous profile). Green: paper-runtime 8,
write-behind 5, tape-store-write-behind 2, tape-startup 5, social-4f-scope 9, publish-fix-96 5.

